1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:19,130
In the Encyclopedia of Triangle Centers, the three Apollonian Circles are the loci of points whose distances to two vertices of triangle A B C are in the inverse ratio of the adjacent sides: P B to P C equals c to b, P C to P A equals a to c, and P A to P B equals b to a.

2
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:29,156
The A-Apollonian circle has diameter I a J a between the interior and exterior angle-bisector feet on side B C, and passes directly through vertex A.

3
00:00:29,156 --> 00:00:35,280
All three Apollonian circles belong to a coaxial pencil whose common chord is the Brocard axis.

4
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:47,540
Their two common intersection points are the First and Second Isodynamic Points X fifteen and X sixteen, where the distances to vertices A, B, and C are strictly inversely proportional to the sidelengths a, b, and c.
